{
  "_readme": {
    "what": "The Latin (English) letter cards. One entry per letter; seed.packs.ts turns each into the `letterSpec` of the abc-<letter> ContentPack. Mirrored in the app at lib/src/features/learn/data/alphabet_latin.dart — change one and change the other.",
    "order": "Teaching order is s-a-t-p-i-n (Jolly Phonics / Letterland), not A-Z. Those first six letters build more real three-letter words than any other six, so a child blends an actual word on day one instead of waiting out an alphabetical march to S. The app offers A-Z as a display toggle.",
    "phoneme.say": "The ONLY string ever handed to a voice engine for the letter sound. Never the glyph: reading a bare 'b' gets you the letter NAME ('bee'), and writing it 'buh' gets you a schwa, which demonstrably breaks blending later. So continuants are HELD ('sss', 'mmm', 'fff'), short vowels are written as the sound ('aaa', 'ehh'), and stops — which physically cannot be held — use the repeated clipped form ('t-t-t', 'b-b-b'), the standard phonics convention. Stops are the hardest case for any synthesizer; when one comes out wrong, an admin overrides that single letter by setting letterSpec.phoneme.audio to a human recording, and the app prefers it automatically.",
    "word rules": [
      "Primary/most-common sound only - no C-for-circle, G-for-giraffe, S-for-sugar.",
      "No initial blends or clusters - a child cannot hear /t/ inside /tr/, so no 'tree' for T, no 'star' for S, no 'block' for B.",
      "No coarticulation traps - 'ant' nasalizes the vowel, 'airplane' is r-controlled, 'elephant' reads as /l/, 'umbrella' reads as 'um'. Prefer apple, egg, up, igloo, octopus.",
      "Concrete picturable nouns only - every word has to survive being a single unambiguous picture.",
      "Every word needs an emoji even once it has art, so a fresh install and an offline device are never blank."
    ],
    "X": "X is taught in FINAL position (box, fox, six). It says /ks/ and practically never starts a word a four-year-old knows.",
    "hi": "Hindi word text ships even though availableLangs stays ['en'] - the story narration isn't translated, but a Hindi-locale child should still read सेब under the apple. A real Devanagari varnamala track is its own script ('devanagari'), not a translation of this one."
  },
